absent: see ''absent'', ''a.''] '''1.''' The state of being absent; the state of being away or not present: as, speak no ill of one in his ''absence.''
{{smaller block|Say, is not ''absence'' death to those who love?
{{right|''Pope'', Autumn.}}}}
{{smaller block|We see on the lip of our companion the presence or ''absence'' of the great masters of thought and poetry to his mind.
{{right|''Emerson'', Domestic Life.}}}}
'''2.''' The period of being away or absent: as, an ''absence'' of several weeks or years.—'''3.''' The state of being wanting; non-existence at the place and time spoken of; want; lack: as, the ''absence'' of evidence.
{{smaller block|In the ''absence'' of conventional law.
{{right|''Chancellor Kent.''}}}}
'''4.''' Absent-mindedness; inattention to things present: a shortened form of ''absence of mind.''
{{smaller block|To conquer that abstraction which is called ''absence.''
{{right|''Landor.''}}}}
{{smaller block|For two or three days I continued subject to frequent involuntary fits of ''absence'', which made me insensible, for the time, to all that was passing around me.
{{right|''B. Taylor'', Lands of the Saracen, p. 147.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Absence of mind,''' habitual or temporary forgetfulness of, or inattention to, one’s immediate surroundings.—}}

