'''II.'''† ''n.'' One who is not present; an absentee.
{{smaller block|Let us enjoy the right of Christian ''absents'', to pray for one another.
{{right|''Bp. Morton'', To Abp. Usher.}}}}

'''absent''' (ab-sent′), ''v. t.'' [< F. ''absenter'' = Sp. Pg. ''ausentar'' = It. ''assentare'', < L. ''absentare'', cause to be away, be away, < ''absen(t-)s'', absent: see ''absent'', ''a.''] To make absent; take or keep away: now used only reflexively, but formerly sometimes otherwise, as by Milton: as, to ''absent'' one’s self from home; he ''absented'' himself from the meeting.
{{smaller block|<poem>If thou didst ever hold me in thy heart,
''Absent'' thee from felicity awhile.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, v. 2.}}
<poem>What change
''Absents'' thee, or what chance detains?</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., x. 108.}}}}

'''absentaneous'''† (ab-sen-tā′nē-us), ''a.'' [< ML. ''absentaneus'', < L. ''absen(t-)s'', absent: see ''absent'', ''a.''] Relating to absence; absent. ''Bailey.''

'''absentation''' (ab-sen-tā′shọn), ''n.'' [< ML. ''absentatio(n-)'', < L. ''absentare'', make absent: see ''absent'', ''v.''] The act of absenting one’s self, or the state of being absent. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|His ''absentation'' at that juncture becomes significant.
{{right|''Sir W. Hamilton'', Discussions, p. 229.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''absentee''' (ab-sen-tē′), ''n.'' 1. One who is absent; more narrowly, one who withdraws from his country, office, estate, post, duty, or the like. {{smaller|Specifically applied, generally by way of reproach, to landlords and capitalists who derive their income from one country, but spend it in another in which they reside.}} 2. In ''law'', one who is without the jurisdiction of a particular court or judge.

'''absenteeism''' (ab-sen-tē′izm), ''n.'' The practice or habit of being an absentee; the practice of absenting one’s self from one’s country, station, estate, etc. {{smaller|''Absenteeism'' in France, under the old ''régime'', was one of the greatest evils, and a prominent cause of the first revolution; and in Ireland it has been a cause of much popular discontent.}}
{{smaller block|Partly from the prevailing ''absenteeism'' among the landlords, . . . these peasants of the north [of Russia] are more energetic, more intelligent, more independent, and consequently less docile and pliable, than those of the fertile central provinces. ''D. M. Wallace'', Russia, p. 109.}}

'''absenteeship''' (ab-sen-tē′ship), ''n.'' Same as ''absenteeism''.

'''absenter''' (ab-sen′tėr), ''n.'' One who absents himself.
{{smaller block|He [Judge Foster] has fined all the ''absenters'' £20 apiece.
{{right|''Lord Thurlow'', Sir M. Foster.}}}}

'''absente reo''' (ab-sen′tē rē′ō). [L.: ''absente'', abl. of ''absen(t-)s'', absent; ''reo'', abl. of ''reus'', a defendant, < ''res'', an action: see ''res''.] The defendant being absent: a law phrase.

'''absently''' (ab′sẹnt-li), ''adv.'' In an absent or inattentive manner; with absence of mind.
